easures for Transitive Functions\nL121: \nL122: Sourav Chakraborty, Chandrima Kayal, Manaswi Paraashar\nL123: \nL124: Abstract\nL125: The role of symmetry in Boolean functions $f:\\{0,1\\}^n \\to \\{0,1\\}$ has been extensively studied in complexity theory. For example, symmetric functions, that is, functions that are invariant under the action of $S_n$, is an important class of functions in the study of Boolean functions.\nL126: A function $f:\\{0,1\\}^n \\to \\{0,1\\}$ is called transitive (or weakly-symmetric) if there exists a transitive group $G$ of $S_n$ such that $f$ is invariant under the action of $G$ - that is the function value remains unchanged even after the bits of the input of $f$ are moved around according to some permutation $σ\\in G$. Understanding various complexity measures of transitive functions has been a rich area of research for the past few decades.\nL127: In this work, we study transitive functions in light of several combinatorial measures. We look at the maximum separation between various pairs of measures for transitive functions. Such study for general Boolean functions has been going on for past many years. The best-known results for general Boolean functions have been nicely compiled by Aaronson et. al (STOC, 2021). The separation between a pair of combinatorial measures is shown by constructing interesting functions that demonstrate the separation.\nL128: But many of the celebrated separation results are via the construction of functions (like \"pointer functions\" from Ambainis et al. (JACM, 2017) and \"cheat-sheet functions\" Aaronson et al. (STOC, 2016)) that are not transitive. Hence, we don't have such separation between the pairs of measures for transitive functions. In this paper we show how to modify some of these functions to construct transitive functions that demonstrate similar separations between pairs of combinatorial measures.\nL129: Keywords\nL130: \nL131:   * Computational Complexity\nL132: \nL133: Mathematics Subject Classification 2020\nL134: \nL135:   * cite31†06E30†zbmath.org - Boolean functions\nL136:   * cite32†20B05†zbmath.org - General theory for finite permutation groups\nL137:   * cite33†68Q25†zbmath.org - Analysis of algorithms and problem complexity\nL138:   * cite34†68R05†zbmath.org - Combinatorics in computer science\nL139: \nL140: Preview\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2512.14643] Improved Lower Bounds for QAC0 (https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.14643)\nciteturn3view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.14643\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 169\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Quantum Physics\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 16 Dec 2025 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 25 Aug 2026 (this version, v4)]\nL9: # Title:Improved Lower Bounds for QAC0\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Malvika Raj Joshi , cite7†Avishay Tal , cite8†Francisca Vasconcelos , cite9†John Wright L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Improved Lower Bounds for QAC0, by Malvika Raj Joshi and 3 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite10†View PDF cite11†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:In this work, we prove the strongest known lower bounds for QAC$^0$, allowing polynomially many gates and ancillae. Our main results show that:\nL17: > (1) Depth-3 QAC$^0$ circuits cannot compute PARITY, and require $\\Omega(\\exp(\\sqrt{n}))$ gates to compute MAJORITY.\nL18: > (2) Depth-2 circuits cannot approximate high-influence Boolean functions (e.g., PARITY) with non-negligible advantage, regardless of size.\nL19: > We develop new classical simulation techniques for QAC$^0$ to obtain our depth-3 bounds. In these results, we relax the output requirement of the quantum circuit to a single bit, making our depth $2$ approximation bound stronger than the previous best bound of Rosenthal (2021). This also enables us to draw natural comparisons with classical AC$^0$ circuits, which can compute PARITY exactly in depth $2$ (exp size).\nL20: Our techniques further suggest that, for boolean total functions, constant-depth quantum circuits do not necessarily provide more power than their classical counterparts. Our third result shows that depth $2$ QAC$^0$ circuits, regardless of size, cannot exactly synthesize an $n$-target nekomata state (a state whose synthesis is directly related to the computation of PARITY).\nL21: This complements the depth $2$ exponential size upper bound of Rosenthal (2021) for approximating nekomatas (which is used as a sub-circuit in the only known constant depth PARITY upper bound). Finally, we argue that approximating PARITY in QAC0, with significantly better than 1/poly(n) advantage on average, is just as hard as computing it exactly. Thus, extending our techniques to higher depths would also rule out approximate circuits for PARITY and related problems\nL22: Subjects:  | Quantum Physics (quant-ph); Computational Complexity (cs.CC)\nL23: Cite as:  | cite12†arXiv:2512.14643 [quant-ph]\nL24:    | (or cite13†arXiv:2512.14643v4 [quant-ph] for this version)\nL25:    | cite14†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2512.14643†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL26: Journal reference:  | STOC 2026: Proceedings of the 58th Annual ACM Symposium on Theory of Computing, 2199-2209\nL27: Related DOI (Digital Object Identifier):  | cite15†https://doi.org/10.1145/3798129.3800922†doi.org DOI(s) linking to related resources\nL28: ## Submission history\nL29: \nL30: From: Malvika Raj Joshi [cite16†view email ]\nL31: cite5†[v1] Tue, 16 Dec 2025 17:59:51 UTC (163 KB)\nL32: cite17†[v2] Tue, 20 Jan 2026 17:44:27 UTC (167 KB)\nL33: cite18†[v3] Mon, 29 Jun 2026 00:53:19 UTC (165 KB)\nL34: [v4] Tue, 25 Aug 2026 